      "passage": "<p>In the village of Tan Phu, the day begins long before sunrise. While the morning mist still lies over the rice fields, the sound of knives splitting bamboo drifts through the narrow lanes. Almost every family here makes baskets, trays and lanterns, a craft that has been handed down for more than two hundred years. For the villagers, weaving is not just a job; it is a way of keeping the community together.</p><p>Children learn the craft by watching. At the age of six or seven, they are given short strips of bamboo and shown how to make simple knots. By the time they are teenagers, many can produce a tray that is almost as fine as the ones their grandparents make. Old Mrs Hoa, who is eighty-one, says that patience is the hardest lesson. \"A strip that is cut too thick will break,\" she explains, \"and it cannot be repaired. You have to start again.\"</p><p>However, the craft now faces serious challenges. Plastic containers are cheaper and are sold in every market, so fewer customers choose handmade baskets. Many young people also leave Tan Phu to work in factories in the city, where the pay is more regular. As a result, some workshops have closed, and the lanes are quieter than they used to be.</p><p>Recently, a group of villagers has tried to give the old craft a new life. They have started to sell lamps and storage boxes to cafés and hotels, and they use social media to show tourists how each piece is made. Some visitors now stay in the village for a weekend to join a weaving class. Although the income is still modest, several young people have returned home to help their families. Their hope is that the sound of bamboo will not disappear from the lanes.</p>",
