      "id": "ta-17",
      "part": "ta",
      "passage": "<p>Most people know that a poor night of sleep leaves them tired the next day, yet few realise how much harm it can do over time. Researchers who study sleep have found that adults need between seven and nine hours of rest every night. Teenagers need even more, usually around nine hours, because their bodies and brains are still developing.</p><p>During sleep, the brain does not simply switch off. Instead, it moves through several cycles, each lasting about ninety minutes. In the deepest stage, the body repairs muscles and strengthens the immune system. In another stage, known as REM sleep, the brain is highly active and appears to organise the memories formed during the day. This is why students who sleep well after studying often remember more than those who stay up all night.</p><p>Unfortunately, modern habits work against healthy sleep. Many people check their phones in bed, and the blue light from screens tricks the brain into thinking that the day has not ended. As a result, the body produces less melatonin, the hormone that makes people feel sleepy. Drinking coffee in the late afternoon can have a similar effect, since caffeine may stay in the body for up to eight hours. Even people who fall asleep easily may find that their rest is lighter and more broken than it should be.</p><p>The consequences of sleep shortage are serious. In one study, a group of volunteers was allowed only five hours of sleep per night for a week. By the end of the week, their reaction times had slowed so much that their performance on a driving test was similar to that of people who had drunk alcohol. Other research links long-term sleep loss to weight gain, heart disease and anxiety. Moreover, tired people tend to make poorer decisions, yet they often feel that they are working perfectly well.</p><p>Fortunately, small changes can make a big difference. Going to bed and waking up at the same time every day helps the body clock stay regular, even at weekends. Keeping the bedroom cool, dark and quiet is also important. Experts further advise people to stop using screens at least an hour before bed and to do something relaxing instead, such as reading a book or taking a warm bath. It may take a couple of weeks before the new routine feels natural, but most people notice an improvement in their mood and energy.</p><p>Some people believe that they can train themselves to need less sleep, but scientists disagree. Although a few individuals may feel fine on six hours, most of those who claim to do so actually perform worse than they realise. In short, sleep is not a luxury or a waste of time; it is a basic need that supports health, learning and happiness.</p>",
